## 2. What only the owner does

These are rulings, not tasks. A session or a teammate may prepare the material for any of them; only the
owner performs the act.

| | Act | Why it is the owner's alone |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | **Merges to the default branch** of every repo | The merge is the review. A branch merged without the owner has not been reviewed |
| 2 | **Confirms a verdict gate** — moves it from `DRAFTED` to binding, amends its wording, rules on a return | A drafted gate is not binding until the owner confirms it; a return blocks, it does not decide |
| 3 | **Ticks a verdict-gate box** or writes its PASS / FAIL record | *A tick means verified, not written.* Verification is the owner's act, and a gate is never retried for a better number |
| 4 | **Changes production** — deploys, hosting, database schema, migrations, environment variables, secrets, DNS | Production is shared by real users and by every service; there is no rollback for a leaked secret |
| 5 | **Edits the governing documents** — `CLAUDE.md`, everything under `.claude/context/`, `.claude/briefs/`, `.claude/returns/`, `.claude/skills/`, `.claude/mods/`, `tools/` | These are the rules the code is judged against. *The constitution beats the code*; whoever edits the constitution edits the standard |
| 6 | **Administers access** — repository collaborators, hosting roles, shared credentials | A new developer never does anything administrative; this is the other side of that promise |
| 7 | **Assigns work** — names the roadmap item and the REQ a contributor works on next | One committed outcome per repo per week; a contributor's item is one of them |

## 3. What a contributor may write

Every write is **on a branch, in a pull request, reviewed and verified by the owner before it merges.**
Within that, a contributor's write set is:

| | Where | What | Condition |
|---|---|---|---|
| ✅ | {{#repos}}`{{name}}`{{^last}}, {{/last}}{{/repos}} | Application code and tests | Inside the roadmap item the owner assigned — one REQ per pull request, the item's id in the branch name and the PR title |
| ✅ | `<code repo>/specs/phase-N/` | The triad — `requirements.md`, `specs.md`, `verification.md` — **for the assigned item only** | Requirement text and checks; **never a gate's wording, never a tick, never a status marker**. Any gate they draft is marked `DRAFTED` and carries no authority |
| ✅ | `{{hub.name}}/.claude/context/roadmap.md` | A `▶ <ID> — inbox` note under their own item's row, in the shape [`hub-inbox`](../.claude/skills/hub-inbox/SKILL.md) gives | Only when their work finds something another repo must act on. The note is the one thing they write in the hub, and it too arrives by pull request |
| ✅ | Their own machine | `.env` files, from the owner | Never committed. `git status` before every commit; if `.env` appears, stop and tell the owner |

Everything not in the table is outside the write set. A pull request from a contributor that touches any
of the following is **closed, not reviewed**, with the reason written on it:

| ⛔ | Path or act | Why |
|---|---|---|
| ⛔ | `CLAUDE.md`, `.claude/context/*` other than their own inbox note, `.claude/briefs/`, `.claude/returns/`, `.claude/skills/`, `.claude/mods/`, `tools/`, `onboarding/` | §2.5 — governing documents |
| ⛔ | `specs/` of any item not assigned to them; any `verification.md` box, gate text or status marker anywhere | §2.2, §2.3 |
| ⛔ | Database migrations, schema files, policies, seed data touching production tables | §2.4 — production |
| ⛔ | `.github/workflows/`, Dockerfiles, deploy configuration, `.env*`, anything holding a secret | §2.4 — production and CI are how the rules are enforced |
| ⛔ | Any commit whose author is not their own identity | §4 — attribution |
| ⛔ | Copy, fixtures, comments or tests that break a binding principle of the [constitution](../.claude/context/constitution.md) §4 or its §5 "NOT" list | The constitution beats the code |

**Reading is unrestricted.** A contributor reads every repository, every document, every brief and every
return. The restriction is on writing, not on knowing.

The `hub-gate` mod enforces the ⛔ paths at the tool call: under a contributor's git identity, an Edit or
Write to a protected path is refused and points here.

## 4. The commit rule

1. **Never to the default branch.** Every change starts on a branch named `<prefix>/<item-id>-<slug>` —
   for example {{#contributors}}{{#first}}`{{branch_prefix}}{{first_prefix}}-<n>-reminder-form`{{/first}}{{/contributors}}{{^has_contributors}}`alex/{{first_prefix}}-<n>-reminder-form`{{/has_contributors}} — and reaches the default branch
   only through a pull request the owner merges.
2. **One REQ per pull request.** The PR title starts with the item id and names the REQ. This matches the
   hub's *one requirement per session* rule and keeps each review small enough to actually verify.
3. **Reviewed *and* verified.** The owner merges only after they have both read the diff and run the check
   the requirement names — the test, the checker, the gate. "Looks right" is a review; it is not a
   verification. A PR the author believes finished but the owner has not verified is still open work.
4. **Green before review.** CI red means the PR is not ready. `tools/specs_check.py` runs from every repo's
   CI and must pass.
5. **Own identity, always.** Before the first commit on any machine:

   ```
   git config --global user.name "<your name>"
   git config --global user.email "<the address hub.json has for you>"
   ```

   A contributor's work authored as the owner is a defect and is rewritten before merge. A
   `Co-Authored-By: Claude …` trailer stays; it says which tool typed, not who is responsible.
6. **No force-push to a branch under review.** Add commits; the owner squashes at merge if they want to.
7. **The owner's own commits follow the same shape** — branch, pull request, green CI — in the code repos.
   In the hub the owner may commit to the default branch directly, because they are the reviewer and there
   is nobody else to review them; the record in the commit message is the review.

## 5. Claude Code sessions inherit the person, never widen them

Claude Code is the only writer in the hub tree (`CLAUDE.md` §Who is who). That sentence is about the
*tool*; this page is about the *person behind it*. The two compose:

```
   who is typing            what the session may do
   ──────────────           ──────────────────────────────────────────────
   {{owner.name}} (owner)   ──►  Claude Code  ──►  everything in CLAUDE.md
   a contributor            ──►  Claude Code  ──►  exactly §3 of this page — on a branch, by PR
   anyone else              ──►  Claude Code  ──►  stops and asks before writing anything
```

A session finds out whose it is by reading `git config user.email` in the repository it opened and looking
the address up in `hub.json`: the owner's address applies `CLAUDE.md` in full; a contributor's applies §3
and §4 of this page and refuses the ⛔ rows **even when asked** — the right answer is "that is the
owner's; here is what I would propose", not the write; an unknown address stops.

## 6. What the owner checks before merging a contributor's PR

The verification in §4.3, as a list, so that it is the same every time:

- [ ] The branch is `<prefix>/<item-id>-…` and the PR is one REQ of that item
- [ ] Every commit's author is the contributor's own identity
- [ ] No file in a ⛔ row of §3 is touched — path list read, not assumed
- [ ] No `.env`, no secret, no credential in the diff
- [ ] Nothing in the diff breaks a binding principle or the "NOT" list of the constitution
- [ ] CI green; `tools/specs_check.py` green; the repo's own tests green
- [ ] The check the REQ names was **run by the owner**, not read about, and its result written on the PR
- [ ] No verification box ticked, no gate text changed, no status marker moved by the PR

## 7. Enforcement — what the rules ask of GitHub

This page is the rule. GitHub is where the rule is made mechanical. These are the owner's actions, once
per repository; until they are done, the rule holds only by agreement.

| | Action | Repos | State |
|---|---|---|---|
| E1 | Branch protection on the default branch: require a pull request, require one approving review, dismiss stale approvals on new commits | all | 🔲 |
| E2 | Block force-pushes and deletions on the default branch | all | 🔲 |
| E3 | Require status checks (CI, `specs-check`) to pass before merge | all | 🔲 |
| E4 | Each contributor added as a collaborator with **Write**, not Maintain or Admin | all | 🔲 |

A `CODEOWNERS` file adds something only when more than one person can approve a pull request; with one
reviewer, E1 already routes every PR to the owner on every path. Revisit it the day a second reviewer joins.

## 8. When a contributor leaves

The owner removes the collaborator access (E4 reversed) and any shared credential; the contributor deletes
local `.env` files as a courtesy. Open branches are either merged under §4 or closed with a note. Their
entry leaves `hub.json`, this page is re-rendered, and the previous version stays in git history as the
record of who could write what, and when.
